# ═══ Epics (kit.notes.md 2026-10-19 16:44Z) ══════════════════════════════════
# "On second thought I think we actually need to support epics." Features group
# bottom up (a behaviour is written, then placed); an epic is defined top down
# and reaches in at any depth. He answered the language question at 17:25Z
# (BEH-EPIC-2): an epic is its own block, and features take it as a parent.

feature FEAT-EPICS "Epics"
  stage problem

behaviour BEH-EPIC-1 "An epic is defined top down, then tagged onto whichever features and behaviours it needs, at any depth"
  in FEAT-EPICS
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-19-1644
  priority 3
  pending
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitStages
  then sees region:KitStagesBoard
  contract "it doesn't come in top level and navigate down level by level. An epic might get tagged onto a ux spec which is multiple layers deep because its parents are not specifically important to the epic"
  contract an item keeps its project and parent feature and gains the epic beside them; the project's own navigation is unchanged ("no need to complicate the projects view")

behaviour BEH-EPIC-2 "An epic is its own kind of block, its parent is a project, and a feature names it as one of its parents"
  in FEAT-EPICS
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-19-1725
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitStages
  then sees region:KitStagesBoard
  contract "epics are separate blocks to features. An epics parent is a project. Features can have multiple parents and they can be other features projects or epics"
  contract my assumption: the block is `epic EPIC-… "title"`, and a feature tags it with the `in EPIC-…` line it already uses for a parent feature, so no second tagging keyword is needed
  contract an `in` line naming an epic that does not exist is refused, as an unknown parent feature is
  contract the parser that reads `epic` reaches main before any corpus uses it: kit/hosted takes the corpus from dev, so a keyword on dev alone breaks production (it did on 10-19 with `stage`)
  contract an epic's tags sit apart from the feature's parents, so the project's navigation never changes; an epic takes no lines yet, and a behaviour reaches an epic through its feature
  contract a behaviour's `in EPIC-…` is refused with the fix in the message: tag its feature instead

